You followed a recipe for finding the line that mentions a meeting.
At home the search worked. In another terminal the result was blank.
Which fact will help someone solve the problem: that the machine
is new, a screenshot of the whole terminal, or the exact status of
one command run against one harmless line? This chapter turns
``it didn't work'' into a report that someone else can check.

\begin{objectives}
\item Identify the system, current shell context, and selected tool
without dumping a private environment.
\item Build a minimal reproduction and distinguish normal empty
results from errors.
\item Write a useful report from selected facts, not a raw transcript.
\end{objectives}

\section{Write the symptom before changing anything}
\label{sec:36-symptom}
\index{diagnostics}
Write down what you expected, what happened instead, and the
last action before the difference appeared. ``Search gave no
lines'' is an observation; ``the tool is broken'' is a guess.
If any output files were involved, stop rerunning the command
against them. A second attempt can replace the evidence and
overwrite a useful draft. Work on copies, or better yet on
fictional data with the same relevant shape. Read the command's
documentation and check what its exit statuses mean
(Chapter~\ref{ch:13}). Some nonzero statuses mean a real error;
others describe a normal result without displaying one.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-36/layers.tex}
\caption{Locate the first layer that differs before replacing tools
or changing the machine.}
\label{fig:36-layers}
\VisualDescription{Working directory and shell lookup lead to
selected tool and then to result; each is a separate checkpoint.}
\end{figure}

The context shown in Figure~\ref{fig:36-layers} is not just
which computer you are using. Two tabs on one screen can have
different working directories or search paths. Start with a
few local observations. Use \cmd|sh| syntax for the fixture
below; diagnose a real problem in the original shell as well.

\begin{terminal}
pwd
uname -s
uname -r
uname -m
\end{terminal}

\cmd|pwd| locates your working directory. In \cmd|uname|,
\cmd|-s| gives the system name, \cmd|-r| the system release
as reported by the operating system, and \cmd|-m| the reported
machine hardware name. On Linux \cmd|-r| is the kernel release,
not the distribution release. On macOS it is the Darwin
release, not the usual macOS product version. Do not include a
hostname or an account path merely because you can print it.
If folder names are not relevant to a report, say ``from the
directory containing the example'' instead of pasting
\cmd|pwd|'s output.

\begin{terminal}
printf 'configured shell hint: %s\n' "${SHELL:-not set}"
ps -p "$$" -o comm
command -v grep
\end{terminal}

\cmd|${SHELL:-not set}| expands to the variable if nonempty,
otherwise to the fallback text. \cmd|SHELL| often reflects
the login shell, not the interpreter currently taking input.
In a regular interactive \cmd|sh|, \cmd|$$| identifies the
shell process; \cmd|ps -p| selects it and \cmd|-o comm|
shows command identity, though it may be only a short name.
This does not identify every startup option. Check the
interpreter used to run a script separately: a line starting
\cmd|sh example.sh| asks \cmd|sh| to interpret that script,
even when typed in a Bash or Zsh terminal. \cmd|command -v|
asks which \cmd|grep| this shell would select. Include a
version only after finding the documented way to get it for
that particular tool; Apple or BSD tools may not support
a GNU program's \cmd|--version| option.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.16in}YY}
\toprule
Question & Check locally & Share if relevant\\
\midrule
Where? & \texttt{pwd} and a small listing of the fixture. &
Which relative path was right or wrong, not your home path.\\
Which system? & \texttt{uname} with specific options;
the OS's release tool. & System or product version, not
a machine name.\\
Which shell/tool? & Shell process, lookup, then local
documentation. & Shell family and chosen implementation,
with paths generalized if personal.\\
What happened? & Output, standard error, and immediate exit
status. & The relevant message reproduced with fictional data.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{macOS: distinguish platform, shell, and tool}
\label{sec:36-macos}
\index{macOS!diagnostics}
\index{Rosetta}
Use this read-only example on macOS, not Linux or BSD. The
operating system version people see in About This Mac is not
the version printed by \cmd|uname -r|. Keep both only if the
distinction is relevant.

\begin{terminal}
sw_vers -productVersion
uname -r
uname -m
\end{terminal}

\cmd|sw_vers -productVersion| requests just the macOS product
version. On Apple silicon a process may run as native
\texttt{arm64} or as translated Intel code under Rosetta.
Different terminal sessions on one machine may therefore see
different environments or software. An \texttt{x86\_64} result
alone is not proof the hardware is Intel. If a trusted installed
tool works in one session but fails in another, compare the
shell and the command resolution in both before changing
packages. Apple's documented translation check reads
\texttt{sysctl.proc\_translated}; the read-only command below
queries that key in the diagnostic process. A reported 1
means translated, 0 means not translated where supported.
If the key is unavailable, do not treat an error as a returned
zero or as proof of the machine's architecture.

\begin{terminal}
sysctl -n sysctl.proc_translated
\end{terminal}

\cmd|-n| requests the value alone. The query describes the
execution context of the command that reads it, not every
application on the Mac. Architecture context, tool location,
and the architecture a binary contains are three different
facts. Do not reinstall tools or change Rosetta settings just
to reproduce the check. Ask the local administrator if the
machine has a managed setup.

\begin{terminal}
command -v grep
ls -l /usr/bin/grep
command -v brew
\end{terminal}

On a typical macOS installation \texttt{/usr/bin/grep} is
Apple-provided. \cmd|ls -l| confirms what is at that
pathname without running it. Homebrew can supply other
implementations, sometimes with prefixed command names. Even
if \cmd|brew| is available, it did not necessarily supply
the selected \cmd|grep|. If trusted Homebrew is present,
\cmd|brew --prefix| can report the configured prefix, and
\cmd|brew list --versions grep| can check its installed
formula inventory. Neither tells you which command the shell
selected; sequence and package caveats matter
(Chapter~\ref{ch:35}). Local \cmd|man| pages may also come
from a different installation than the executable. Report
``Apple-supplied grep on macOS'' or the exact alternate
implementation you identified, rather than ``the Mac version.''
On Linux, the distribution information can come from
\texttt{/etc/os-release}, where present. On FreeBSD,
OpenBSD, and NetBSD, use the relevant local system release
manual and distinguish base utilities from optional packages.

If the behavior depends on the session, collect the same
observations in both sessions. Mark which one worked. For
example, in one macOS tab you might select the base tool and
in another a Homebrew tool with a different option vocabulary.
On an Apple silicon machine, you might instead see the same
package name in two different prefixes, each used in a
different architecture context. Neither difference by itself
shows that one tool is faulty. Reproduce the discrepancy with
exactly the same minimal input and an option documented for
both tools, or explain that the option belongs to only one.
A fix can be as simple as using the syntax for the intended
implementation. Keep a record of the working command before
you modify an alias, an environment variable, or a startup file.

If a line fails only when run from an editor, task launcher,
or scheduler, run the diagnosis in that context if the tool
supports a safe test. Report that context in plain language.
``The search command fails in my editor's task window, but
works in a shell started in the same directory'' narrows the
investigation. A terminal window and an editor's display can
look similar and use different command paths. This is one
reason not to replace a working base tool while trying to
correct a single app's configuration.

\section{Reduce the problem until it fits on a card}
\label{sec:36-reproduce}
\index{minimal reproduction}
Create a fresh private practice directory from a writable
location. As in earlier chapters, the non-POSIX but commonly
available \cmd|mktemp -d| creates a unique directory. Stop if
the setup does not print its path. Do not use real notes
or test with a real account name.

\begin{terminal}
room=$(mktemp -d ./utp-report.XXXXXX) && cd "$room" && pwd
\end{terminal}

\begin{terminal}
printf '%s\n' 'Meeting: Tuesday' > ./sample.txt
grep -F 'meeting' ./sample.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|grep -F| treats its pattern as a fixed string. Without
an ignore-case option, \texttt{meeting} does not match the
capitalized \texttt{Meeting}, so this example should print
only \texttt{status: 1}. The immediate status must belong
to \cmd|grep|, not to a later \cmd|pwd| or \cmd|printf|.
A missing file causes a different result:

\begin{terminal}
grep -F 'Meeting' ./not-created.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

No such file was created. Expect an error on standard error
and a status greater than one, not a normal unsuccessful
match. Use the error to direct the next check. If it says
permission denied instead, don't try elevating privileges;
check the pathname and access first. Compare the working
case and a one-option variation:

\begin{terminal}
grep -F 'Meeting' ./sample.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
grep -Fi 'meeting' ./sample.txt
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|-i| ignores differences in case. In these examples both
searches print \texttt{Meeting: Tuesday} with status zero.
When narrowing a real issue, change one feature at a time.
Keep the exact behavior you are investigating when you simplify
the data. If removing a tab, accent, embedded quote, or line
ending makes the problem disappear, restore the relevant feature
with fictional words. If the issue is specific to a locale,
that setting may be relevant, but do not send a whole
environment dump to communicate it.
Figure~\ref{fig:36-grep} shows how the immediate status
adds a distinction that the visible search output cannot
provide by itself.

The fixture above is a useful check if the original tool
was embedded in a pipeline. A later stage could print
nothing because an earlier stage gave it nothing, or it
could succeed with empty input while an earlier stage
failed. Before replacing the last command, take the stages
apart as in Chapter~\ref{ch:17}. Use the tiny sample with
each important stage and record the first one whose
behavior differs from your expectation. If the output
is large, choose the smallest input that still shows
that difference. A pipeline that appears to run more
quickly after a change may simply be doing less work.

\subsection{Capture only the lines needed to explain an error}
For an ordinary manual test you can usually read the screen.
When standard output and standard error have been mixed by a
wrapper, capture them separately in the practice directory.
The names in the next example must be new. Run this only
once in your fresh fixture; \cmd|>| and \cmd|2>| can
truncate existing files. \cmd|>| directs standard output
to its named destination, and \cmd|2>| does the same for
standard error. Neither redirection captures the exit status.

\begin{terminal}
grep -F 'Meeting' ./not-created.txt > ./out.txt 2> ./err.txt
result=$?
wc -c < ./out.txt
cat ./err.txt
printf 'saved status: %s\n' "$result"
unset result
\end{terminal}

Expect zero bytes of output, one file-related diagnostic,
and a saved status greater than one. A diagnostic can differ
with the implementation and locale. If a redirection itself
failed, the shell can report an error before the search
starts, so check the destination first. The file count and
read above are subsequent commands; without the assignment
they would overwrite the very status under investigation.
Compare this with the no-match case, which has no missing-file
diagnostic. Do not just concatenate the two captures into a
report. Select the relevant excerpt, rewrite the case with
fictional data if it isn't already fictional, and verify the
same outcome from that case.

If either file would contain private information on a real
run, don't capture it unnecessarily. Terminal redirection
creates files whose permission defaults depend on the
environment, as Chapter~\ref{ch:33} explains. A transcript
can also be backed up, synced, or retained by tooling. It is
simpler to build the fictional case first and capture only
that. In the practice directory, remove those two generated
files when done. Confirm only the names just created.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i ./out.txt ./err.txt
\end{terminal}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-36/grep.tex}
\caption{A match, no match, and an error are three different outcomes.}
\label{fig:36-grep}
\VisualDescription{Three columns describe status zero with a matching
line, status one with no matching line, and status greater than one
with an error.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.14in}YY}
\toprule
Observation & First useful question & Unhelpful jump\\
\midrule
Status 1; no line & Does the pattern match the actual case
or characters? & Reinstall the search utility.\\
Error on stderr & Is the input path right and can it be read? &
Change permissions on the entire tree.\\
Works in one tab & Are the path, shell, lookup, and
locale comparable? & Treat the machine as a single environment.\\
Different installed versions & Which implementation produced
each observation? & Declare both behaviors interchangeable.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Share a rebuilt report, not a dump}
\label{sec:36-report}
\index{diagnostics!redaction}
A good report lets a person repeat the experiment without
getting access to your files. Start with the fictional
fixture and the exact command, then expected and observed
results. Include the circumstances that change behavior.
Say what you did not test. For example, ``This occurs when I
use the lowercase pattern in this shell; I didn't test another
implementation'' is more useful than ``every grep is broken.''
If you tried an administrative action, say so: it might have
changed the very conditions you are trying to reproduce.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-36/share.tex}
\caption{Rebuild the shared report using selected, sanitized evidence.}
\label{fig:36-share}
\VisualDescription{Private notes are used to select facts, then a
fictional reproduction becomes the report. No arrow bypasses selection.}
\end{figure}

Leave secrets out from the start. Environment dumps, shell
history, process arguments, logs, package inventories, and
screenshots can contain account names, internal network
locations, document titles, tokens, and credentials. Even an
image of one error can show another tab, a prompt, or a path.
If you need to describe a private path, create another path
with the same relevant features, such as a space, instead of
trying to cover characters on a screenshot. Inspect the
entire outgoing text, including quoted replies and any
attachments, from the recipient's point of view. If a secret
was accidentally disclosed, changing the post may not remove
copies; use the account or service's revocation procedure and
contact the responsible administrator if needed.
The separation in Figure~\ref{fig:36-share} is intentional:
your local working record and the document you send need
not contain the same material.

\subsection{What makes a reproduction actionable?}
Imagine a tool maintainer is reading the report at a different
computer. They first need to know how to create the example,
then exactly what to type, then what to look for. Include the
setup command with its trailing newline if that matters;
show the line count or byte count if the input shape is the
problem. Don't include optional aliases or a long chain
of steps if the failure also happens without them. If a step
requires a particular shell, package implementation, or
operating-system version, state that before the command.
If the reader can run the whole example twice without
losing data, you have made it much easier to test and discuss.

Separate findings from possibilities. ``It failed for a
path with a space. The same command worked after quoting the
path'' is a useful pair of observations. ``It is definitely a
shell bug'' is a diagnosis you have not established merely by
seeing that difference. List a working comparison as well as
the failure; this provides a check that the shared fixture
is shaped correctly and that the recipient is testing the
same thing. If you can't reduce the problem without private
inputs, explain where you had to stop and ask for a safer
way to provide evidence.

When the problem is intermittent, add how often you can
reproduce it and what appears to distinguish successes
from failures. ``It fails after I resume my laptop but not
in a fresh session'' is more useful than a dozen identical
error excerpts. If it appears only during heavy work,
inspect the destination and relevant process status without
trying to recreate that load on a shared machine. If your
account is managed, note that fact and ask for approved
ways to verify policy rather than attempting to defeat it.
In a report that crosses teams, time and time zone may
be enough to locate an event; do not send a full log by
default. If the problem might include another person's
data, stop collecting it and ask where evidence should go.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Does more output make a better report?}
\Brian{Wouldn't they fix this faster if I sent my whole terminal history?}
\Stephen{They'd have to find the relevant lines first.}
\Brian{But what if I accidentally omit the clue?}
\Stephen{Keep notes locally and start with a repeatable example.}
\Brian{And if they ask for a log that includes customer names?}
\Stephen{Ask which fields matter and whether fictional data shows the same issue.}
\Brian{So I can be precise without handing them my environment.}
\Stephen{Yes. Precision is mainly about controlling the example.}
\DialogueConclusion{Share the evidence, not the environment.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\begin{exercise}{Find the first difference}
\label{ex:36-difference}
Within the practice directory, compare a fixed-string
search for \texttt{Meeting} against a fixed-string search
for \texttt{meeting}. Keep everything else the same.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd|grep -F 'Meeting' ./sample.txt| and inspect the
status immediately with \cmd|printf '%s\n' "$?"|. Repeat
with the lowercase pattern. Expect a line and zero, then
no line and one. No data was changed.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Distinguish silence from error}
\label{ex:36-status}
Recreate the missing-file error, then a no-match result.
What fact distinguishes them when standard output is empty
in both cases?
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd|grep -F 'Meeting' ./not-created.txt| and immediately
print \cmd|"$?"| with \cmd|printf|. Repeat for
\cmd|grep -F 'Wednesday' ./sample.txt|. The first command
normally has a diagnostic and status greater than one; the
second has status one and no match. Neither creates files.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Write a report someone can try}
\label{ex:36-redact}
Write a four-line note containing a fictional input, exact
command, expected result, and observation. If it matters,
add the search utility identity without disclosing personal
paths. Do not post the report.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
For example: ``One line: Meeting: Tuesday. Command:
\texttt{grep -F 'meeting' ./sample.txt}. Expected the line.
Observed no line and status 1.'' If you discovered that the
command performs a case-sensitive search, add that
\texttt{-i} changed the result. Review the note for real
names or paths and keep or discard the note locally.
\end{solution}

Check \cmd|pwd| before removing the fictional input.
\cmd|rm -i| asks for confirmation. The directory removal
will refuse to delete any additional files you left there.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i ./sample.txt
cd .. && rmdir "$room"
unset room
\end{terminal}

\begin{takeaways}
\item Record a symptom and the relevant context before changing the system.
\item Use a fictional minimal reproduction and the command's exact status.
\item Share just enough for another reader to repeat the test.
\end{takeaways}

